Release 0.1.3.

Changelog: 28-10-2026 15:18:36

Fixed. The pre-push hook checked every suite's versions and manifest on every push, so a Windows Portable Suite
left in a failing state would have held a push of the BC250 suite, where its CI and its tags never would (owner,
28-10-2026: the suites stand apart). tools/pre-push.sh now runs a suite's checks only when the push changes that
suite (its folder; the root's copy of apps.toml counts for the suite it copies), check_versions.py --suite for each
such suite and --nexus (core, dev/) when none changes; a new branch still checks every suite
(tools/tests/test_pre_push.py). Nothing GitHub runs changes: a suite's tags and release workflow already waited for
its own CI and Nexus's only. The Nexus page's Development chapter and CLAUDE.md say so.

Changelog: 28-10-2026 15:08:07

Fixed. CI Windows Portable's steps "Release: test" and "Release: portal" read as a release of the Windows Portable
Suite while the BC250 suite's portal was released (owner, 28-10-2026; on the Actions page the run was titled with
the push's commit message, Release portal-v12.1.1, too). They only build the release zip on GitHub's test machine
for the installer test after them, and publish nothing: they are called "Build the release zip, not published:
test" and "...: portal" now (windows-portable/tools/tests/test_installer_tests.py follows). The Nexus page's Releases
chapter says that only a tag builds and publishes a release, and why every run of a release push carries its title.

Changelog: 28-10-2026 14:48:47

Release 0.1.2.

Changelog: 28-10-2026 14:32:15

Fixed. The push of Nexus 0.1.1 made nexus-index.yml fail (owner, 28-10-2026): the release tags ruleset still
protected nexus-v0.1.0, so the run deleted that release and then could not delete its tag, and GitHub's Latest fell
back to the newest app release. A ruleset that only excludes nexus-v* does nothing beside the one that includes
*-v*: the exclusion belongs in that ruleset (the Nexus page, GitHub settings, says so now). The workflow also
removes every index tag left without its release (git/matching-refs/tags/nexus), so such a run leaves one index
again once the ruleset allows it, where it only removed the tag it was about to make.

