Changelog: 15-10-2026 07:22:42

CI runs again for every push to a branch: ci.yml's push filter is branches: ["**"] instead of tags-ignore. GitHub
reads a push filter with only tags-ignore as "no branch", so the previous change ran CI for no push at all (5166f51
and 2de5db6 had none, and tag-release.sh rightly tagged neither). A tag still starts no CI. Tests:
tools/tests/test_ci_local.py reads the trigger as GitHub does (a branch filter, no tag filter) and checks every job's
time limit.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 06:59:56

The wiki's GitHub settings page has a section on the GitHub CLI (gh) with the commands a release needs: the runs of a
push or of one commit (--commit needs the full SHA), whether CI passed (tools/ci_state.py), the log of a failed or
hung job, canceling and running a run again, running every stopped release on a commit again at once, and reading a
release's files.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 06:49:07

CI no longer runs for a tag (ci.yml: push with tags-ignore). The release of 22 tags on one commit started 22 more CI
runs of the same jobs: about 780 jobs queued behind GitHub's limit of about 20 at a time, and every release waited
(release.yml's ci job) for all 23 runs, so one flaky test failed all of them and one hanging job held them for an
hour. tools/tag-release.sh already tags only a pushed commit CI passed, and release.yml waits for that commit's CI.
Every CI job has a time limit (timeout-minutes, 10 to 45, far above their usual minute or two), and release.yml's ci
job 80 minutes, so a hang fails instead of running for GitHub's six hours. Release archives of Bazzite Test are
reproducible again: tools/build_release.py gives build_info.py the commit time (SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH), so the build date
no longer depends on the clock; test_reproducible failed when its two builds fell either side of a minute. The wiki's
portal chapter says so. Tests: the build date in the archive is the commit time.

