Changelog: 15-10-2026 04:42:09

Only a commit that CI passed becomes a release, and CI can be run locally as GitHub runs it. tools/ci_state.py asks
GitHub whether every CI run on a commit passed (passed, failed, running, none; waits while CI runs; signs in with gh
when it can). tools/tag-release.sh asks it before tagging and waits while CI runs, up to an hour; release.yml has a
first job, ci, that does the same before anything is built or published (up to 75 minutes), so a tag on a failing
commit gives no release. tools/ci-local.sh runs the jobs of .github/workflows/ci.yml in a container (tools/ci-
local/Containerfile: Python 3.12 and what GitHub's runner has, libevdev included) with tools/ci_local.py: each job,
and each matrix entry, in a venv with only its own requirements, as the jobs run as an ordinary user on a staged copy
of the checkout; the jobs come from ci.yml itself, the Windows job is reported as not run. About 75 seconds for every
job once the image and Console Link's FFmpeg are built (kept in ~/.cache/bc250-ci-local). It catches the failures of
the last releases before the push: a Python 3.13 function (read_text(newline=)) and a test that needed a package only
the full venv had. pyyaml joins tools/requirements-dev.txt. The wiki's Development page and the portal chapter
describe it; CLAUDE.md lists tools/ci-local.sh first among the checks. Tests: tools/tests/test_ci_state.py,
test_ci_local.py and test_tag_release.py (a failed or running CI gets no tag).

